# The platform's own default StorageClass — managed by homelab-infra.
#
# A class of our own rather than an edit to the one k3s ships, for two reasons. The first
# is that Kubernetes forbids it: `reclaimPolicy` is immutable, and patching k3s's
# `local-path` answers `Forbidden: updates to reclaimPolicy are forbidden`. The second is
# that k3s re-stages and re-applies its packaged addons on every start of the service, so
# an edit to one of them survives only until the next restart — which is exactly how this
# cluster ended up with two default classes and no error to show for it.
#
# It is the ONLY default class on this cluster, and that is now true by construction rather
# than by patching: `local-storage` is on the disable list in the node configuration, so
# k3s's class does not exist here at all. tasks/storage.yml asserts on every run that
# exactly one default class exists and that it is this one.
#
# The provisioner name is the same string the bundled class used, because the provisioner
# is the same program — this platform runs its own copy of it, declared in
# local-path-provisioner.yaml.j2. PersistentVolumes record the provisioner that created
# them, so this string is what keeps volumes made before the switch claimed by the
# controller running after it.
---
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: {{ k8s_cluster_config.storage.class }}
  annotations:
    storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"
    # Stated on the object itself, because this is the property most likely to be
    # forgotten by whoever reads `kubectl get sc` two years from now and sees a
    # three-node cluster.
    homelab-infra.io/description: >-
      Node-pinned. A volume is a directory on one node's disk and pins its pod to that
      node; if the node is unavailable the pod stays Pending rather than rescheduling.
      The control plane is highly available. Data on this class is not.
provisioner: rancher.io/local-path
reclaimPolicy: {{ k8s_cluster_config.storage.reclaim_policy }}
# What the local-path provisioner expects. Binding at first consumer lets the scheduler place the pod
# before the volume is created, so the volume lands on the node the pod actually got
# rather than pinning the pod to a node chosen before scheduling.
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
